WEBVTT
TITLE Multivalued Dependencies And 4nf — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Pythagoras has two children and two salary-years, so the table holds four rows for him.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Children and salary history are independent facts, and the flat table writes every combination.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
The cost is c times s rows: three children and two salary-years give six rows where five would do.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
No functional dependency is violated: the only key is all three attributes, so this is BCNF.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
X ->> Y says: once x is fixed, the set of Y-values does not depend on which Z-value you also fixed.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
Equivalently: two tuples that agree on X may swap their Y-parts, and the results must still be in the relation.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
Both readings are tested against each other on 4,864 cases: every relation on three attributes over a two-value domain.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
They agree in all 4,864. The definition has two faces, and they are one definition.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Every functional dependency is an MVD: 753 instances here satisfy X -> Y and therefore X ->> Y.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The converse fails in 1,915 cases: two tuples with the same x and different y break the FD but not the MVD.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
An MVD is trivial when Y lies inside X, or when X and Y together are the whole schema.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
And X ->> Y always brings X ->> Z with it: complementation, checked on every case.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Fagin's theorem: X ->> Y holds exactly when the relation is the join of its XY and XZ projections.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
4,864 cases tested, the MVD held in 2,668 of them, and the join recovered the relation in exactly those.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Delete one tuple from the employee table and the MVD dies with it: eight rows become seven.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
So an MVD is a claim about every legal instance, not a fact about one printed table.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Fourth normal form: for every non-trivial MVD X ->> Y, the set X is a superkey.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Employee, Child, Salary has no FD at all, so BCNF is satisfied and 4NF is not.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
So 4NF is strictly stronger than BCNF, and this schema is the witness that the converse fails.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The key here is all three attributes, which is why BCNF has nothing to complain about.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
To fix it, split on the violating MVD: X together with Y, and X together with Z.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The pizza table: 12 rows become 6 and 6, and joining them back returns exactly the 12.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
A new variety for A1 now costs one row instead of three, and the half-done update is impossible.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
The split is lossless by construction: the fragments share X, and the MVD is what makes the join exact.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Four implications from a past paper, each decided by search over all 256 instances.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
A ->> B with A ->> C does not give A -> BC: the full cube of eight tuples satisfies both MVDs.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
A ->> BC with A -> B does not give A -> C: four tuples satisfy both premises and break the conclusion.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The other two hold, by union, decomposition and replication: two of four valid, so the answer is 2.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Three or more fragments need the chase: a tableau with one row per fragment and a distinguished symbol per attribute.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Functional dependencies unify symbols; a multivalued dependency adds the swapped row.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
AB with ACD works: the MVD produces a fully distinguished row. AB, AC and AD does not.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
More fragments is not more safety: four tuples in, eight tuples out.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
An MVD is not a multivalued attribute: 1NF is about cells, 4NF is about rows.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
BCNF constrains functional dependencies; 4NF constrains multivalued ones, which BCNF cannot see.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
4NF is not 5NF: 5NF constrains join dependencies, which is the next rung and a different question.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Papers list 4NF as an option even when the answer is 3NF, so read what the option actually asks.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: find the keys from the functional dependencies alone.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: list the non-trivial MVDs, and test each left side against the superkeys.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: split on a violating MVD into X with Y and X with Z, then re-test each fragment.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: three or more fragments, or a test against implied MVDs, means chase it.

